Can you provide a minimal working example or at least code for both models?

My understanding is that blavaan allows specifications that can produce non-PD matrices by rejecting them until one is found. Since the PD share of valid matrices is incredibly small in some cases, perhaps a matrix wasn't found within constraints of the sampler? I would imagine that would throw an error. Similarly it could just be a warning that it came across a non-PD matrix.

In any of these cases, comparing Bayesian and non-Bayesian models without really knowing how informative the priors you are using are on the entire matrix doesn't seem like a particularly good idea - presumably you'd want to compare with more or less informative priors. You could try the equivalent of prior predictive checks to see how it works out but I imagine it will be hard to figure out due to the complex interdependencies among the univariate priors. One approach that could be more transparent is found here ttp://srmart.in/informative-priors-for-correlation-matrices-an-easy-approach/ <http://srmart.in/informative-priors-for-correlation-matrices-an-easy-approach/>

